00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:03.370
Well, with all of that,

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.740
thank you so much for
joining us here today.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.639
And I'm so excited to be joined
by my co-host, Mike Lopez.

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:14.560
So Mike is senior director of
data and analytics at the NFL,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.985
National Football League.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.530
And Mike, I'd love to have
you introduce yourself,

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.940
maybe tell us something you
like to do outside of work too,

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.785
and share a little
bit about your role.

00:02:23.785 --> 00:02:24.880
- Thanks, Rachael.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.940
And thanks to everyone to
Posit for organizing this.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.355
It is an exciting time
to be in football data.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.715
And we just have a lot of
questions that we have to try and use

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:43.150
naive or advanced models for
because there is so much about

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.400
the world of football that
we are just starting to learn.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.390
My background was, I was
a high school teacher, turned

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.340
PhD student, turned assistant
professor at college.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.435
And at that point,

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.750
the influx of football
data became apparent.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.640
The league started
getting Next Gen Stats,

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.120
which is each player wears a pair
of RFID chips in their shoulder pads.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:08.580
And you went from an NFL
data history where every game had

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.390
160 plays, so 160 rows of data,

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:14.420
which everyone was analyzing
locally on their laptops to

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.910
now, a world where we have
this tracking data, both

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:21.620
RFID-based and then now even
video-based. And it's just hard--

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.020
there's just a lot that we
are learning and trying to figure

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:28.220
out. So my job is to organize
a group of really intelligent

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.770
young data scientists that
are helping us and helping the

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:31.715
league make better decisions.

00:03:31.715 --> 00:03:33.530
- Awesome. Thanks, Mike.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.392
And something you like to
do outside of work too. [LAUGHS]

00:03:38.365 --> 00:03:41.674
- Out of work. Our preseason
starts tonight. And

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:48.800
I now realize that from now
until the end of March, most of

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.470
my spare time is going to
be keeping an eye on what happens

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:53.315
and everything
related to the NFL.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:55.010
So I got my--

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.710
my spare time was over the
summer. I have three young kids,

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.550
and so hanging out with them is
really how I spend my spare time.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.200
But tonight,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.545
that is now done with an eye
on the NFL game that's on the television.

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:10.025
- So watching football, is
what you do in your spare time?

00:04:10.025 --> 00:04:10.760
- Yeah.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:12.820
It's

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.150
a nice side gig.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.250
- That's great. Well, Mike,

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.470
I know there'll be a lot
of questions coming in from

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.100
everybody shortly here.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.965
But just hearing that initially about
how you're using data from the RFID

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:26.720
tags on the players,

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.780
I'd love to just understand a
little bit more about what that

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.225
process looks like and how you

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:35.285
ingest that data, what
you do with that data?

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.260
Just walking me through a
little bit of that process.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:43.130
- So the league has, I
would say, two data warehouses of

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:46.190
systems of storing
data. In the early 2000,

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.380
that's when the league
initially started building a

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.295
lot of the SQL server that the clubs
still use and that we would still use.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:54.470
That would be your
traditional game stats.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:55.790
So if you play
fantasy football,

00:04:56.450 --> 00:05:00.320
the yards caught by a wide
receiver, or the quarterbacks

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.800
yards per game, or
whatever that would be,

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.830
that would be your
traditional game stats.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.360
In 2015 was when
the league first--

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:12.340
it might have been '14 or

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.400
'15. I wasn't with the
league at that point.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.770
But that's when the league
started collecting the player tracking data.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:21.080
And they had seen it catch
on in other sports and it was

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.270
driven by the media
group out in Los Angeles.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:24.740
And so if the

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:26.990
game stats are
stored in New York,

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.420
the media data is stored
and managed by Zebra,

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:30.965
and the Next Gen
Stats team out in LA,

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:32.810
we ingest it.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:35.090
There are a couple of ways
that the league gets it.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.203
One is using an API.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:41.450
The second is there is a
SQL server that now is dedicated to

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.295
organizing and
managing that data.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.840
One of the unique things about
the tracking data is that it

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.720
really changes your data
pipeline and how you organize

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.800
and manage your code because

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:56.870
you used to be able to
just load every NFL game

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:01.460
play-by-play, whatever it has, and
analyze it, fit a model,

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:04.595
make a graph. Now, you have
to be a little bit more

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.290
articulate about how
you're doing that--

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:08.580
I don't know if articulate
is the right word.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:09.740
But you have to
be a little bit--

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.270
be forward-thinking.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:14.210
And a lot of times, what we
do is just fit it on a play.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.330
If you want to figure out,
was the wide receiver open?

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.760
Don't try to do that on
every play that wide receiver has

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.290
ever done. Just try to do it
on one play. And identify a way of

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.920
whatever metric you're creating or
looking at openness for that

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:29.340
wide receiver, and

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.700
doing it on that play.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:34.880
And that takes a lot
of time because you're probably the

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.890
first person to think about
your level of ranking,

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:38.060
that openness or whatever. And

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:41.420
we're doing a lot of things
at the league to increase the

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.300
awareness of football data and to
give people the code so that

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:47.585
they can build their new work
off of it, but it's still new.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:50.210
And then once you've
done it on that one play,

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:51.800
you then try and
do it at a game.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.930
Try to write a function
to do it on the game.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.590
And then obviously, extract it
to all players and all games.

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.245
And all that stuff takes
time. So that's how

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.220
the traditional--

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.220
you would be an analyst in
the year 2012 or '13, and

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.750
you'd download CSVs, and you'd
analyze all the plays at once.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:12.840
Now you have to be a little
bit more cognizant about how to do

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.900
that just because of the
size of the tracking data.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.750
- Thank you. That's
really helpful.

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.840
I see a ton of
questions starting to come in here.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.390
Devin, I see you asked one in
the chat. Do you want to jump

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.640
in? I can ask the question.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.710
If you want to jump in with
more context, feel free.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.450
But the question was,

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:31.020
what kind of decision-making is
your work driving at the league

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:36.060
level versus the Moneyball
team versus team decision-making?

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:39.590
And Devin said, thanks for
powering my fantasy leagues as well.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:40.325
- So

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.700
we are primarily interested
in what happens on the field.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:45.690
And if it happens on the field,

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.240
we're hoping to measure
it in the sense that--

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.370
how close are our games?
How exciting are they?

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.510
What are the trends in
how teams are calling plays,

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.580
and how players are playing,
and how coaches are coaching?

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.610
We do a lot
with our officiating group,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.990
trying to help them
make better decisions.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:04.800
And then also both the
on-field decisions,

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:05.670
but also the training.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:09.660
We work with player health and
safety on making sure that the

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:12.465
rules of the game are
the best match between

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.550
keeping the players safe,

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:20.250
making sure that their equipment is
as top notch as it can be.

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:23.460
And then also going back to
the rules, like what are the best--

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.820
whenever we talk about
a rule of the game, it's

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.490
driven by does it make
the game more competitive?

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:33.480
Does it make the game
safer? Can our officials officiate it?

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:34.590
Does it keep the game exciting?

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:38.739
Things like that. So we try
to organize a lot of our support

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:41.909
for the league around a
lot of those questions.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:45.780
The teams make the decisions
to go for it on fourth down.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.015
They make the decisions
to challenge a play,

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:52.140
to start at quarterback or
to draft a certain player.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:55.260
But a lot of what we're trying
to help with is that there are

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.180
new tools to help
with those decisions.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:59.280
There's new metrics
that we can help create,

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.350
there's new data sets that
we can provide them. And

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:04.750
ultimately, what they do with
it is obviously up to them.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:08.650
- I know you just touched
upon the health data. But Geraldine,

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:10.190
I saw you had a
question about that.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.400
Do you want to ask
a follow-up there?

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.170
- Yeah. Because when
I asked that question,

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.820
you were mostly talking about
tactics and plays, et cetera.

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:17.980
So I was wondering,

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.890
do you guys also
analyze players'

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:23.245
health stats around things like

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:27.100
whether or not they get
concussions from impacts or

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:31.040
dehydration? Or is there a
separate team that deals with that?

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:31.125
-

00:09:31.125 --> 00:09:33.520
So as part of the NFL
collective bargaining agreement

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:35.170
with the Players Association,

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:37.820
the league doesn't analyze
that data themselves,

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.560
but there are two
third parties that do.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:43.030
Two companies that
we work with a lot,

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:45.670
IQVIA and Biacore
are their names.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:47.200
And they're both really good,

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:49.900
from an epidemiological side
and an engineering side,

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:52.105
at looking at how do we--

00:09:52.105 --> 00:09:54.520
what are the
trends in injuries?

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.400
What are both rates
of concussions or injuries,

00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:59.170
or missed time injuries,
or leg strains?

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:02.230
How have those changed over
time as athletes have gotten

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.885
faster and the teams
are passing more? And

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:06.580
things like that.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:08.995
So those are collaborations
that we're a part of.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.450
The specific raw injury data is
not something we touch on a lot.

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:17.680
Only in the sense that when
it comes time for changing the

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.090
punt player, the kickoff play,

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:22.660
or the rules around
how to protect the quarterback,

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:24.700
it's never just a health
and safety question.

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.520
It's health and safety and
an officiating question.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:29.740
And a

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.190
competitive question about if this
is going to make our games

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:36.280
closer, or more blow outs,
or less exciting, or less

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.480
tradition, things like that.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:41.740
So a lot of the way that we
try to work with those types of

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:43.600
questions is like,

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:47.050
how does this impact the
entirety of the game? And

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:51.010
the other aspect that I think
is really neat about where

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:54.620
football is now is that
it's not just the game data,

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:55.880
it's also the practice data.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:56.755
And so

00:10:57.820 --> 00:10:58.320
most

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.990
clubs have performance
scientists that work with

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:05.565
trainers on managing
player load and

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.970
optimizing player
performance from, I think,

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.140
both a performance
and an injury side.

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:14.070
And that's not stuff that
we're allowed to help with,

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:17.160
but we do know that a lot
of clubs are going down that route.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.570
- Greg, I see you had
a question in the chat too.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:20.445
Do you want to jump in here?

00:11:22.005 --> 00:11:25.210
- Yeah, sure. Thanks,
Michael for being here today.

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:27.900
I actually got a chance to
see you speak at the University of

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:29.590
Denver back before
the pandemic,

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:32.070
which seems like a lifetime
ago at the Sports Analytics Conference.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:36.410
So I was just wondering if
you have any thoughts on how transferable

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:39.140
analytics skills--

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:42.120
sports analytics specifically
are across leagues.

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:46.650
If you build up experience
with baseball or basketball,

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:49.390
how does that transfer
to football or other sports?

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:51.330
I know some of them are
more emerging than others.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.640
- I mean, honestly,

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.130
I don't think you can
be a great sports data scientist

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:58.425
without really having a sense of
what's going on in other sports.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:02.050
So I would say, it's
really two-fold. One is

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:04.930
the skills themselves
are transferable.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:06.640
If you can code tracking
data in football, you

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:10.160
can code it in baseball, you can
code it in basketball, et cetera.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:14.005
It might take you a little bit of
time to get up to speed, but the specific

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:17.200
functions that you're writing
or the specific ways that

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.570
you're transforming
the tracking data into

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:23.550
metrics that you can
communicate with a coach, that

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:26.485
you can do in any sport
once you can do it in one.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:28.780
The hard part is being
able to do it in one.

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:31.240
So I think that's one area.

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:33.500
And then the second one,

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:36.670
which is really maybe
just as important is,

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:39.070
what are the other sports
doing? What are they looking at?

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:43.270
One recent thing that's
caught on to a lot of folks in

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:46.165
football is that
baseball teams no longer

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:49.780
draft a player and then
send the player out there,

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:51.550
and the player performs
or doesn't perform.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:55.210
They're now doing things
in training to maximize the

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:59.410
player's launch angle, or their
exit velocity, or their swing

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:04.220
path because they think that they
can take a player and help

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.540
them improve using data.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.750
And I don't think football
has always been that way.

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:10.010
I think

00:13:11.714 --> 00:13:13.070
maybe corollary would be,

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:15.530
you would have a quarterback
and you'd throw the quarterback

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:18.080
out there, and they'd either
complete the pass or miss it,

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:20.120
and maybe you could get a
new offensive coordinator.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:22.580
But maybe now, you
could be thinking about,

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:23.975
where is the quarterback
holding the ball?

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:26.450
What is their path to throw it?

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:28.760
Is there something
that we can do that's

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.670
maybe the corollary of
a swing in baseball?

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.750
So that's one example that
isn't certainly the only example,

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:39.140
but I think it's a combination
of both the hard skills,

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:42.230
hands-on keyboard stuff that
if you can code, you can code.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:43.190
And if you know sports,

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.890
I think that's going
to certainly transfer.

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:48.140
But there's also an awareness
of what are other leagues

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.855
doing? What are the metrics
that they've come up with?

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:52.940
I think

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:56.330
it certainly helps on the
data science to have that background

00:13:56.330 --> 00:13:59.180
and that skill set because
there's just so much out there

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:00.740
that the NFL can learn
from other sports.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:03.910
And I do think specifically
given some of the competitions

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:06.190
and the work that the football
has done in the last couple of

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:09.060
years, other sports are now
learning stuff from football too.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:10.900
- I appreciate that.
Thank you very much.

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:11.620
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Mike,

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:15.070
we talk a lot about
community in the Data Science Hangout.

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:18.310
And I think the Big Data
Bowl is an amazing community

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:20.395
initiative that
stands out to me.

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:22.960
And I was wondering,
do you see this

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:27.400
initiative happening in other
sports or other industries now?

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:30.820
And do you have any tips
for people who are trying to do

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:31.585
something similar?

00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:35.905
And it might be helpful to just
explain what the Big Data Bowl is too.

00:14:35.905 --> 00:14:36.580
- So

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:40.300
one of my weaknesses
is I hold grudges.

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:45.970
And so I used to always get
a little bit irritated with some well-known

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:50.365
sports, conferences, and
organizations that would

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:55.540
maybe run hackathons or
share data in ways that,

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:58.060
as an academic and as somebody
who supports reproducible

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:00.920
research, maybe didn't
have those same values.

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:04.730
And so when I started at
the league, I was pretty steadfast,

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:07.136
that I thought the league could
do a better job of sharing data.

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:11.510
And one of the ways that
we've done that is through an event

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:15.330
called the Big Data Bowl, and
it has a couple of purposes:

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:19.340
One is let's innovate in
football. And we can be

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:22.970
talented all we want as
a league office and whatnot,

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:26.090
but there's only so much that
our group can do and the Next

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:28.647
Gen Stats group can do. There's
just a lot of other things.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:32.850
And the idea being that
there's just a lot we can do.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.490
And if you share data,
people analyze it.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:35.530
So the

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:37.640
quote from Field
of Dreams is,

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:38.810
"If you build it,
they will come."

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:41.840
If you share the data,
they will analyze it,

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.765
especially in sports
data. So that's

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:46.860
one of the main
reasons we did it.

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:51.260
We also have the motivation
where we have this complex data

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:52.560
set that no one's
ever looked at,

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.680
and at least this was
our initial philosophy in 2018.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:57.920
And if that's how we feel,

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:00.270
that's also how the
32 NFL teams feel.

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:02.510
And when you work
at a league office,

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:06.510
you're in some ways
employed by the 32 teams.

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:07.640
And so we're doing it for them.

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:11.300
We're doing it to give
them a pipeline of analytics staffers

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:14.420
that they can hire to go
answer their own questions.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.150
And one of the really neat
things is, and I've hinted at

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:18.140
this, is that this

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:21.650
is an opportunity for anyone
to come in and analyze.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:25.220
And it really levels the
playing field in the sense that

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:28.733
before, when you would hire in

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:32.750
sports data, you
might look at a GPA,

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:35.210
or you might look at where
the person went to college,

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:36.485
or you might look at

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:41.210
maybe where they
interned last summer.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.100
Now, you're looking
at their code.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:45.230
You're looking at
what they built.

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:48.140
You're looking at what is
their level of thoughtfulness in the

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:50.870
questions and the way
they communicate their results?

00:16:50.870 --> 00:16:51.740
Which ultimately,

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:53.690
that's how you're evaluated
when you're on a team.

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:56.480
If you're on a data
science team and that's your job,

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.705
is to build interpretable
metrics and to communicate those,

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:02.860
and to do a good
job of validating your models,

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:03.730
and things like that,

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.780
that's what the Big
Data Bowl is all about.

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:06.280
So

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:08.020
we've seen a lot of--

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:11.200
one of the neat things is
that now people are copying

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:13.449
football, and that was never
a place where football was before.

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:16.179
There was the Big Data
Derby and horse racing.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:18.250
There was the Big Data Cup,

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:21.939
which athletes
put on in hockey.

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.743
I think there was like now
a fantasy football one.

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:29.690
And it's pretty humbling
to know that that

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:34.210
largely started with the NFL
sharing data and being open

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:38.920
about it. So we're certainly
open to more leagues doing it.

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:40.465
In terms of advice,

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:42.240
the

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:47.380
number one thing that we
found is that the crowdsourced

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:50.200
metrics are always better than
anything we've ever come up

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:51.450
with ourselves. And

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:55.030
the more thoughtful and,

00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:58.620
I guess,

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.540
thinking forward about
what people will analyze,

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.730
I think companies will
get the most out of it.

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:08.970
We've had competitions that
have immediately turned into

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:11.525
metrics that are
shared on air. And

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:18.750
our 2020 competition was on
rushing yards and within maybe

00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:21.060
11 months from the
contest ending,

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:25.620
you watched the NFC title game,
which has maybe 30 million

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:26.880
viewers. They're

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:32.100
watching a metric that
came from our competition. And

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:33.770
that's a

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:37.140
really exciting aspect
of that competition.

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:39.120
And so the better
questions you ask,

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:40.230
the better answers you'll get.

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:42.540
But I think overall,

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:46.650
we've certainly found that the
crowdsourced approach has really helped.

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:47.850
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
That's amazing.

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:49.410
I was just sharing in the chat,

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:53.160
last year's winners actually
spoke at a Posit meetup.

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:56.250
So if anybody wants to save
that and check it out too.

00:18:57.800 --> 00:19:00.260
Yue, I see you've had your
hand raised for a bit here.

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:01.210
Do you want to jump in?

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:01.885
- Sure.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:05.420
Hi, Michael. Thank you
for being here. I have one

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:09.260
question. So what type of data
are you using for the analysis?

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:12.770
Are they tabular structured
data or unstructured data,

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:16.350
like images or recording
from the game directory?

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:18.320
And if the data
are unstructured,

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:22.625
how do you process them before you
can use them for analysis and modeling?

00:19:22.625 --> 00:19:25.460
- Good question.
I would say, with

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.400
each passing year, we're
getting to more unstructured

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:31.470
data. But I think the league
has done a pretty good job.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:32.780
And when you're at
a league office,

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.570
we have a large number of
data engineers that help--

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:39.020
that ensure our SQL tables are
clean and easy to read from.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:41.510
I would say, most of
it's pretty structured.

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:44.880
And the other reality is
with player tracking data.

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:46.265
At least in the NFL,

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:51.723
because it's RFID-based, it's very accurate.
And so it's a tabular format.

00:19:52.410 --> 00:19:54.050
In fact, if you go to
our Big Data Bowl site,

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:56.850
you can download it as a
CSV. And it's a big data set,

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:57.780
but it comes out clean.

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:01.380
That's not necessarily the case
with maybe some of the newer

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:03.810
data sets we'd be getting
into. If you talk about,

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:05.505
like video tracking,
which would be

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:09.290
taking from an image and then
trying to look at player location.

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:14.190
One of the ways that football
is going is that we have RFID

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:18.075
tags on all 32 NFL teams and
all the players and all the games.

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:22.470
However, those players come
from a pool of college players.

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:26.250
The NFL can't make college
players wear those same RFID tags.

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:28.590
And so when you
want college data,

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:30.930
you either have your
traditional stats or you've got

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:32.260
to use video-based data.

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:35.685
And so a lot of
that video-based data on college players

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:39.750
is stuff that is
a little messier.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:41.910
And so that would be where
you would have to extract it from

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:43.290
imaging, and things like that.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:45.330
There are companies
out there that do that.

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:46.460
And

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:49.480
I don't know if
it's an arms race,

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:51.870
but I do know that NFL
teams are really interested in those

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:54.330
skills because
of the way that--

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:55.965
before, you would have to have

00:20:57.070 --> 00:21:00.250
a dozen scouts go out and watch
videos of the teams in the

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:01.470
northeast and the southeast

00:21:02.290 --> 00:21:05.150
and all over. You'd send
scouts. And teams still do that.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:08.000
But now, if you can get
some of that insight by

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:11.740
coming up with an algorithm
that can take the data from the

00:21:11.740 --> 00:21:15.040
video and turn it into
a nice, usable format,

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:17.290
it can certainly save you some
time and maybe get you some

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:18.700
insight you weren't
getting otherwise.

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:21.490
- I see so many great
questions coming in here.

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:25.240
So I'm just trying just to
sort through some of them. But Lisa,

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:27.840
I see you had a
question a bit earlier

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:31.840
on Mike's transition
from academia to the NFL.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:32.870
Do you want to jump in?

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:33.370
- Sure.

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:35.155
I'm Lisa.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:40.540
I was just curious about
how you made the transition from

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:41.940
academia to

00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:48.343
working for the NFL and how
you think that academic experience was helpful?

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:51.460
And then maybe, again,
this is probably selfish,

00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:55.330
but how did you market
that academic skill set?

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:58.310
- I think the best part about--

00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:02.390
the most helpful part about being
in academia is that you are evaluated--

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:04.520
and I was at a small
liberal arts college--

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:06.080
you're evaluated
on your teaching.

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:07.085
So you have to be
a good teacher.

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:11.255
I was a tenure-track
assistant professor, and

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:13.970
evals would come in. And when
you weren't doing a good job,

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:16.250
communicating to your students, that's not--

00:22:18.146 --> 00:22:19.530
that's going to ding
you or whatever.

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:20.030
So

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:22.430
I think that's the
most helpful skill set,

00:22:22.430 --> 00:22:24.815
is that my job was to take

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:30.140
statistical methods and distill them down
to students who were learning it.

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:34.130
I've been teaching
R for maybe--

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:34.630
or

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:37.860
some software for a decade.

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:42.350
But when you do that in 2020
or whatever, I couldn't be

00:22:42.350 --> 00:22:45.080
teaching the same stuff I
was teaching in 2010 because

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:46.770
software was
entirely different.

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:48.810
So you also have
to keep up-to-date.

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:54.320
And that's a lot of what we
do now. Our job is to communicate

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:58.790
statistical results or methods
to folks that aren't statistics

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:03.050
experts. Now instead of
students, now it's executives.

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:07.880
One of the things that we
are constantly working on is,

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:11.660
what do we show? How much is
too much? When do we lose them?

00:23:11.660 --> 00:23:13.460
How many plots is
too many plots?

00:23:13.460 --> 00:23:15.455
Do they need to know the
ins and outs of our models?

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:19.460
And whereas in when
you're teaching,

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:22.190
you really need to communicate
all the ins and outs.

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:23.450
Certainly, we
don't do that now.

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:25.730
But I think there's a lot of--

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:27.950
the experience that was most
helpful was teaching and

00:23:27.950 --> 00:23:28.820
communicating results.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:31.460
We don't do as much writing.

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:35.120
I don't have as many research
papers maybe as I used to would

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:36.605
have. But now,

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:38.390
for better or for worse,

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:41.060
I spend a lot of time
making presentations.

00:23:41.060 --> 00:23:43.820
And I think a lot of that's
the same approach where you're

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:47.920
trying to take what you've
learned and distill it into a

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:50.840
template that others
can understand.

00:23:51.380 --> 00:23:51.880
I

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:55.990
started in 2018 when I
worked at the league office, maybe

00:23:57.110 --> 00:24:01.070
doing one PowerPoint deck
every couple months. And now,

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:02.840
for better for worse, I just--

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:05.780
I have to do that a lot
because I have a really talented group

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:08.885
of data scientists that are coming
up with so many cool things. And

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:16.160
I am now helping them
curate material to share with executives.

00:24:16.160 --> 00:24:16.660
And

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:21.995
like I mentioned at the start, there's
a lot that we can do and

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:26.660
trying to figure out how
to communicate that goes back to

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:27.830
that experience in academia,

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:29.855
where you're writing a journal
article or you're writing

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:32.870
a summary of your
work. So that's

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:35.720
where it's been most valuable.

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:36.590
And I also think

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:41.135
one of the cool aspects about
where we are now is that

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:43.940
there's just still
a lot to learn.

00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:46.820
And that's no different than
being in academia where you're

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:49.970
an expert in something and
you're trying to build the next

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:53.360
tool or you're trying to build
the next model or the next paper.

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:53.975
And there's--

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:56.600
I think it's not that--

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:02.070
I like to often refer to our
work as we have a laundry list,

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:04.290
and sometimes our job is
to just start at the top,

00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:05.550
what's the most important
thing we can do,

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:07.680
and that's not much different
when you're in academia and

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:09.480
trying to do the same
thing. So great question.

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:10.560
And I don't know if
I answered it right,

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:11.970
but that's what I would think.

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:14.580
- Yeah. That was great. I
took some notes. So thanks.

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:15.570
- Of course.

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:16.950
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:19.260
Can we stay on this topic
for just a bit here?

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:20.160
When you were saying--

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:22.350
when you were talking about
the communication skills that have

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:23.580
transferred over,

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:27.480
I was curious if you do
have certain tips for us for

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:32.100
communicating to executives or what
you've learned in many of

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:33.720
those presentations
you've given?

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:35.880
- I mean, we've tried
and failed a lot. I mean,

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:38.490
there's things that I've pushed
for or we felt strongly about

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:40.095
that other people
were like, yeah.

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:42.960
And then there's other things
where maybe I didn't feel as

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:45.390
passionate about. And then
suddenly, maybe every--

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:49.530
maybe the presentation got
shared a lot more widely and I

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.090
read about it the
next day on espn.com.

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:54.600
So it's hard to really know.

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:58.960
I haven't kept a spreadsheet of
what's worked or what hasn't in

00:25:58.960 --> 00:26:00.850
terms of communicating
results. I think

00:26:02.578 --> 00:26:05.530
we have to have good
data visualization. If we don't,

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:07.090
we can't do our job.

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:08.350
And

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:11.260
I think I spend probably--

00:26:11.260 --> 00:26:14.050
and my team would probably complain
about how I do this. But

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:16.450
I probably complain
about things that I--

00:26:16.450 --> 00:26:18.085
if this was a student paper,

00:26:19.210 --> 00:26:21.730
I probably wouldn't write because
I have to make sure

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:25.330
that the work that is
being presented is as clear and

00:26:25.330 --> 00:26:28.390
concise as it possibly can
be. It can't be confusing.

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:30.080
It has to have the
right annotations.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:33.580
The stuff that has come out
in ggplot in the last couple of

00:26:33.580 --> 00:26:37.780
years and also in gt with the
visualizing the tables has

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:40.645
been really helpful in that
front. Because for a while,

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:43.975
we would have to make tables
in an old school format

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:48.100
or I'd have to add annotations
in PowerPoint because you

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:49.390
couldn't code that.

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:51.880
So that's probably the
most important thing,

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:53.980
is your viz has to be topnotch.

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:56.550
I also--

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:59.700
and this just comes partly from
experience partly because I

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:02.190
played football in college and
my dad was a football coach,

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:05.010
and I grew up watching
way too much film.

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:08.580
But the more closer you are
to the subject level matter,

00:27:08.580 --> 00:27:09.825
the better you are at your job.

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:12.030
It doesn't mean that you
need it for all roles.

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:15.840
You can be a great coder and
learn that skill set along the way.

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:19.620
And I mean, football is,
in some sense, a complicated

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:21.930
sport. But in another,

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:24.420
anybody can learn it
if you try to learn it.

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:27.795
So it's not like it's too
far away from somebody to learn.

00:27:29.070 --> 00:27:31.110
My mother-in-law complains
that it's too hard to learn.

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:34.350
But I would say, in general,
you can figure this stuff out.

00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:38.290
But a lot of the things that
we do is we just answer their questions.

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:38.790
And

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:41.070
I think the

00:27:42.756 --> 00:27:45.540
second part of communicating that
result is sometimes we are

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:47.850
answering the questions that
we know they're going to ask

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.860
before they've asked
them. And I think

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:54.880
the best presentations we give
are the ones where they're

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:57.400
asking a question and
then just click Next,

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:58.300
and it's on the next slide.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:03.220
Or they ask a question and
I say, yep, it's in the Appendix.

00:28:03.220 --> 00:28:05.800
Click a couple slides
forward and say, here it is.

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:07.930
Because you have to anticipate
the questions they're going to

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:08.860
ask. You have to--

00:28:08.860 --> 00:28:09.430
sometimes,

00:28:09.430 --> 00:28:11.770
we're collecting data before they've
asked us to collect data

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:13.900
because we can tell--

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:16.855
like we hear something in the
media or we hear something that

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:21.160
they're asking in the replay
room or their coaches are

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:24.040
asking, and we're like,
we're going to go build this.

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:28.060
Because they didn't tell us to
go collect data on quarterback slides,

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:30.670
but we now know that
quarterbacks are sliding more

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:31.490
than ever before.

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:33.535
We know quarterbacks are the
most important position in football.

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:34.930
So last year,

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:38.080
Andrew Patton basically led
an NFL data collection processing

00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:39.070
quarterback slides.

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:41.530
Because we knew that when
we got to the offseason,

00:28:41.530 --> 00:28:43.035
somebody was going to say, hey,

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:45.940
how are how are quarterbacks
sliding? And it's like,

00:28:45.940 --> 00:28:48.350
actually, we have that because
we went out and collected it.

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:51.130
So I would say that it's a
combination of great data viz

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:55.420
and then also being
purposeful and forward-thinking with the

00:28:55.420 --> 00:28:58.540
questions that people are
going to be wanting to ask.

00:28:59.115 --> 00:29:01.180
- That's great. Thank
you so much, Mike.

00:29:03.075 --> 00:29:05.190
Sanjay, I see you had your
hand raised a little bit ago.

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:06.230
Do you want to jump in here?

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:07.520
- Yeah. Hi.

00:29:08.660 --> 00:29:09.160
I

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:11.270
was curious when you said RFID

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:14.460
tags were used
to track players.

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:16.535
And so what all the
data do you collect

00:29:17.570 --> 00:29:20.510
apart from just tracking the
players? I mean, is that speed,

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:23.960
direction? What all is
captured? Just curious to know.

00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:27.350
- So it captures
the x, y-coordinate data, two

00:29:27.350 --> 00:29:28.715
dimensional. We don't
get their height.

00:29:29.540 --> 00:29:32.885
The tags are like the
size of your thumbnail

00:29:33.530 --> 00:29:35.150
and they fit into the
player shoulder pads.

00:29:36.050 --> 00:29:38.690
And so the players don't
even know that they're wearing them.

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:40.580
They turn on whenever
they go on the field.

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:43.670
You could watch players on
the sideline if you wanted to.

00:29:43.670 --> 00:29:44.540
Wouldn't recommend it.

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:45.040
But

00:29:46.340 --> 00:29:50.120
then the league takes that
data, they share. From the x,

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:52.760
y data, we get their speed
acceleration because it's two

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:54.960
chips. We also get
their orientation.

00:29:55.760 --> 00:30:00.195
That's all curated and sent out
to the teams from the league office.

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:01.820
So

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:08.100
you could watch NFL and
you'd see fastest players or fastest

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:09.240
acceleration speed.

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:13.540
You could get change of direction
from it without too much effort.

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:15.930
So that's the type of thing
that you would get from this data.

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:17.805
- And do you have an
RFID on the ball also? MIKE LOPEZ

00:30:17.805 --> 00:30:18.305
: Yes.

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:21.555
Good question. Yeah,
there's an RFID on the ball.

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:22.950
With players,

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:26.190
I would say it's accurate
usually to within a couple

00:30:26.190 --> 00:30:27.990
inches. The ball is maybe--

00:30:27.990 --> 00:30:30.060
has a larger margin of error,

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:32.310
maybe closer to
five or six inches.

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:37.320
And then with all of it,
with both the 22 players, the ball,

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:41.250
there's also RFID tags in
things on the sideline like the

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:44.820
pylons and the chains. I don't
think there's much use in

00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:45.900
those, but the league

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:47.730
has been collecting it.

00:30:49.020 --> 00:30:51.360
And then it's all
at a frames of

00:30:52.650 --> 00:30:53.625
10 frames per second.

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:54.740
So

00:30:56.880 --> 00:31:01.140
a given a play is seven
seconds long, you're going to get 70

00:31:01.140 --> 00:31:03.240
observations of that
player on that play.

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:05.820
And you could animate it
to follow them around.

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:08.220
You also get their data before
the snap and after the snap.

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:09.795
It's just a matter of
what you find most useful.

00:31:09.795 --> 00:31:10.620
- Aaron,

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:13.170
I think you had a question
related to that about the ball

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:15.920
too. Do you to jump in
here? I think the question

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:21.210
was about officiating.
And let's see. Aaron said,

00:31:21.210 --> 00:31:23.820
I've always wondered why can't a
tracker be placed on the ball

00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:28.080
to determine when it crosses the
plane for a first down or touchdown?

00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:31.260
So is that something
that's being looked at?

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:33.825
- And I think Geraldine's
question is similar.

00:31:34.410 --> 00:31:35.520
It is being looked at.

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:38.655
There are a couple of differences
that I think matter a lot.

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:44.250
One is, NFL stadiums are
enormous and there's a lot of

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:46.480
player overlap on
almost every play.

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:48.150
And so the cameras have
to be further away,

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:50.100
unlike tennis where
they're right on the field.

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:53.870
And unlike maybe a VAR decision
in soccer where there's two or

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:55.890
three players maybe
right in a tight space,

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:57.280
arguably there a lot more.

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:01.020
So the technology that you can
see in other sports is a little

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:04.870
bit more complex to
apply to football. In my

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:06.953
now six years,

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:09.960
there have been
several attempts,

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:13.320
some gaining more traction than
others to do video-based spotting.

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:17.400
It is a challenge.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:17.900
And

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:22.650
the current accuracy margin of
error of the football itself

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:25.190
using the chip in the
middle of the ball can't

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:26.630
replace

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:30.540
the on-field spotting for
a couple of reasons. One,

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:32.045
I mentioned the
margin of error.

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:37.200
If you're saying
the ball is here,

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:39.630
but then you look on the
film and it's actually here,

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:42.720
it's really difficult to justify
that to a viewer at home,

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:46.155
why you're making a first down decision
or a decision on the goal line.

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:50.400
And I think the second
part there too is the

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:54.000
ball spotting process in the NFL is
based on the tip of the football.

00:32:54.630 --> 00:32:55.130
Without

00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:59.680
totally changing the construct of
the chip in the football,

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:01.645
we don't know that, and the
chip is in the middle of football.

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:04.630
It's been tried to
put multiple chips in,

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:08.620
then you're messing around with
the mechanics of how that feels

00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:10.705
to the quarterback. And
we don't want to do that.

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:13.450
So there are
video-based attempts,

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:16.240
that my intuition is
within a couple of years,

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:20.005
there will be use cases of
it that you'll see on air where

00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:25.405
the technology is helping the
process of spotting the football.

00:33:26.470 --> 00:33:30.370
But it's not as easy as
maybe it appears in the other sports.

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:31.330
And I also think

00:33:33.760 --> 00:33:37.255
the other aspect for us is
that pace of play is really important

00:33:38.740 --> 00:33:41.890
in the sense that tennis can
have a break or soccer can have

00:33:41.890 --> 00:33:43.720
a break to look
at this decision,

00:33:43.720 --> 00:33:45.460
and maybe the ball
isn't in play.

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:48.865
The second the ball is put
down, the ball is in play.

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:55.030
Other than the couple stoppages
for replay or in between downs,

00:33:56.530 --> 00:33:59.500
in between a fourth down
and a punt play or whatever,

00:33:59.500 --> 00:34:00.820
there's no

00:34:02.600 --> 00:34:06.410
example of saying, pause, hold
on, we're all going to wait.

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:08.810
We just don't have that time.
Coaches want the ball to move.

00:34:08.810 --> 00:34:10.850
We want the game to
be three hours or so.

00:34:10.850 --> 00:34:14.900
And that continued
focus means, sure,

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:17.389
there might be a tool out there
that's going to get us the

00:34:17.389 --> 00:34:21.469
exact right spot. But if
it takes three minutes, that's not

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:24.289
a trade-off I think the
league would be making.

00:34:24.289 --> 00:34:25.234
- Thank you.

00:34:25.834 --> 00:34:28.699
I see Jared had a question, and
you put the star next to it.

00:34:28.699 --> 00:34:30.560
So I'll read it.
But Jared asked,

00:34:30.560 --> 00:34:34.400
how does someone get into the
NFL space as a data scientist

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:37.246
to help improve player
safety and performance?

00:34:37.246 --> 00:34:41.380
- So I think there's a couple
of ways. One is, I mean, there

00:34:42.380 --> 00:34:42.920
are--

00:34:42.920 --> 00:34:44.239
I mentioned IQVIA and Biacore,

00:34:44.239 --> 00:34:46.310
but they are two companies
we work with a lot.

00:34:46.310 --> 00:34:47.930
And they're
specifically targeting

00:34:49.310 --> 00:34:53.044
things like the epidemiology
of injuries, return to play,

00:34:53.780 --> 00:34:57.455
injury trends based on
how teams are playing.

00:34:58.250 --> 00:35:01.010
The equipment, safest
helmets, safest cleats,

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:03.020
safest shoulder pads, or

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:09.610
the characteristics within a
play that the orientation of

00:35:10.430 --> 00:35:10.930
player

00:35:11.750 --> 00:35:13.460
limbs that could
lead to injury.

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:17.720
So I think there are things
that are specific to the injury

00:35:17.720 --> 00:35:18.880
side of things that

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:20.700
are part of it.

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:23.370
Then there's also the maybe
more performance side,

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:27.020
which is all the teams
want those same questions answered.

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:28.430
And they have--

00:35:28.430 --> 00:35:31.880
just like you see the Next
Gen Stats player tracking data in games,

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:36.330
they get catapulted in practice
or there are other companies

00:35:36.330 --> 00:35:37.620
besides catapult
that do it does it.

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:39.360
But catapult is probably
the biggest one.

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:43.890
So you have the same data that you
get on your own players in practice. Now,

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:46.330
that's not data that the
league office would get.

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:49.950
But if you're on a team
and you could identify, like listen,

00:35:49.950 --> 00:35:53.460
this player has had two or
three really hard days in a

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:56.340
row, maybe that comes
with an increased injury,

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:59.760
risk of a hamstring strain
or something. I'm just--

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:02.730
I'm not necessarily making
that up, but that's hypothetical.

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:05.340
So those would be the things
that you would get into,

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:08.890
is thinking along like,
what happens within a practice?

00:36:08.890 --> 00:36:12.420
And how to make sure that
that player that put forth a certain

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:14.010
load, that traveled
a certain distance,

00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:18.490
that ran at his
high speed for 10

00:36:19.230 --> 00:36:21.580
minutes, which is maybe
a lot, or three minutes,

00:36:21.580 --> 00:36:22.620
which maybe isn't a lot,

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:24.240
how are they going to
perform the next day?

00:36:24.810 --> 00:36:25.570
Because ultimately,

00:36:25.570 --> 00:36:28.380
the job of teams is to
win games and you win games when

00:36:28.380 --> 00:36:29.260
your players are healthy.

00:36:29.260 --> 00:36:31.740
So the coaches understand
the value of this too.

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:33.910
So it's a league level basis.

00:36:33.910 --> 00:36:36.100
But then also on a
more micro level,

00:36:36.100 --> 00:36:37.570
the teams are also
looking into it.

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:39.310
- Thank you. Nick,

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:41.990
I see your hand was raised
just a few seconds ago.

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:42.850
Do you want to jump in here?

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:44.320
- Yeah. Sorry. Sure.

00:36:45.580 --> 00:36:48.190
Coming up in here. Hi,
everybody. Nice to talk to you.

00:36:48.190 --> 00:36:50.110
So my question revolved around,

00:36:50.890 --> 00:36:52.120
I guess, communication

00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:58.630
of what you're finding and just having
some a personal feel and read on football

00:36:59.800 --> 00:37:01.150
personnel and staff.

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:04.060
So I know a lot of the
old guard guys might be--

00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:06.670
and folks in general might
be a little bit resistant to

00:37:06.670 --> 00:37:10.070
analytics and just the
philosophy. So I was wondering--

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:13.360
and I know you mentioned
you're doing more PowerPoint

00:37:13.360 --> 00:37:15.160
presentations and
talking to people.

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:17.605
How are you getting people to

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:21.280
buy into analytics as a
whole? How can you take

00:37:22.040 --> 00:37:25.510
the modeling that's the math
and turn it into actual advice,

00:37:25.510 --> 00:37:27.710
but also from a
perspective that is

00:37:28.510 --> 00:37:30.940
digestible for football
coaches, or personnel,

00:37:30.940 --> 00:37:33.730
or whoever might be working
with you in a way that they can

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:37.240
use it, accept it, and
feel better about it?

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:38.860
Because around the
football space,

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:41.170
it seems like the word
analytics is a little bit like

00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:43.090
a stink on it, if
you know what I mean.

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:45.790
- I haven't had a
lot of pushback.

00:37:47.590 --> 00:37:48.520
I've been here six years.

00:37:48.520 --> 00:37:50.380
There's not been a
single meeting where somebody was

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:51.580
like, I don't want
to see your results,

00:37:51.580 --> 00:37:54.800
or I don't care about
y, x, or z. I mean,

00:37:54.800 --> 00:37:56.740
I mentioned earlier that there
are certainly times where I've

00:37:56.740 --> 00:37:59.680
thought the league maybe could
have done this or a certain

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:01.100
team could have said, hey,

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:02.440
we think we should
prioritize this more,

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:03.640
and maybe that hasn't happened.

00:38:04.600 --> 00:38:06.340
I think a lot of
it comes back to,

00:38:06.340 --> 00:38:08.710
we're not trying to answer
different questions that they are.

00:38:08.710 --> 00:38:10.540
We're trying to answer the same
question they are and we're

00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:11.710
trying to put numbers to it.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:12.900
And

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:17.860
there are lots of examples where
we just get a sense of

00:38:17.860 --> 00:38:19.990
the questions that they're
after, what they're interested

00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:23.170
in. And we say, here's
what we're thinking.

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:24.100
And I also think

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:27.970
I think we do an appropriate
job pushing where we need to

00:38:27.970 --> 00:38:31.630
push and then also
saying sometimes, listen, I mean,

00:38:31.630 --> 00:38:34.210
there are a couple examples
of random projections we've been

00:38:34.210 --> 00:38:38.365
working on in the last week
where sometimes we've just said honestly,

00:38:39.040 --> 00:38:41.920
we're giving you a projection
for something for some time.

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:44.980
But I wouldn't even
really trust it.

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:47.950
I personally don't trust our
own model because there's too

00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:49.720
many uncertainties that--

00:38:49.720 --> 00:38:51.850
too much noise in the system.
So I think we're also--

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:52.615
we try to balance

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:54.670
when we push.

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:55.170
And

00:38:55.930 --> 00:38:58.180
one of the advises I got
from somebody, I think,

00:38:58.180 --> 00:39:00.820
in hockey a couple
of years ago is--

00:39:00.820 --> 00:39:04.780
I was asking that person
why their team didn't do something.

00:39:04.780 --> 00:39:06.430
And it was like, OK--

00:39:06.430 --> 00:39:07.180
in hockey,

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:10.000
it was a specific thing that I
thought teams could do to gain an edge.

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:14.500
And their point was, this
analysts that I talked to, is

00:39:14.500 --> 00:39:17.680
that they look at their role
as they have a certain amount of

00:39:17.680 --> 00:39:22.040
times that they could really
push. And sometimes, you do it.

00:39:22.040 --> 00:39:23.440
And sometimes, you say, listen,

00:39:23.440 --> 00:39:27.100
I think my team probably could
increase my chances of winning by this,

00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:31.270
but there's something else that comes with
it that maybe I don't want to--

00:39:31.270 --> 00:39:32.470
it's not necessarily--

00:39:32.470 --> 00:39:35.180
it moves the needle only this
much and it's not worth it.

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:37.600
So I do think our team also
tries to be a little bit

00:39:37.600 --> 00:39:39.085
purposeful with how we balance

00:39:40.060 --> 00:39:42.200
when we are saying, like, hey,

00:39:42.200 --> 00:39:44.200
this is something we should
change or this is something we

00:39:44.200 --> 00:39:48.165
should do because we only
get so many cracks at it. And

00:39:49.300 --> 00:39:51.380
there are times
where I think hey,

00:39:51.380 --> 00:39:53.470
we could really improve
our decision-making here.

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:56.110
And there are other times
where I would say, hey, listen,

00:39:56.110 --> 00:39:58.780
I can give you a number,
but go with your gut.

00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:00.200
It's probably not going
to be that far off.

00:40:00.200 --> 00:40:00.700
So

00:40:01.240 --> 00:40:04.450
I think our team is
pretty smart about adding all the

00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:07.030
caveats and the
stars to say, hey--

00:40:07.030 --> 00:40:07.960
the asterisk--

00:40:07.960 --> 00:40:10.870
like hey, take our results
with a grain of salt.

00:40:11.440 --> 00:40:14.230
So many things are happening
in football. Teams are changing,

00:40:14.230 --> 00:40:15.790
the rules are changing that.

00:40:15.790 --> 00:40:19.270
Assuming that maybe this is going
to hold or that this is

00:40:19.270 --> 00:40:21.670
the best way to do it
is not right. But then other times,

00:40:21.670 --> 00:40:24.760
we try to really push for
things. So it's a balance of that. RACHAEL

00:40:24.760 --> 00:40:26.515
DEMPSEY: That's really
helpful feedback.

00:40:27.610 --> 00:40:30.310
Listening to all the
different pieces of

00:40:31.030 --> 00:40:33.870
data you're working with
or problems you're looking at

00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:37.680
solving, even some things that
people haven't asked for yet,

00:40:37.680 --> 00:40:40.485
how do you prioritize what
your team is working on?

00:40:42.375 --> 00:40:44.100
- I don't have a
great answer there.

00:40:47.240 --> 00:40:49.620
A lot of it is honestly--

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:50.120
we

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:54.630
have a meeting in a week
or so with some executives.

00:40:54.630 --> 00:40:57.630
And my last slide is, here are
six things we can do this fall.

00:40:58.230 --> 00:41:01.215
Tell me where you would
prefer we spend our time.

00:41:02.020 --> 00:41:05.100
So maybe that's part of this,
is just getting a read on it.

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:09.780
We're not totally obtuse to what
we hear in the media in

00:41:09.780 --> 00:41:12.165
terms of what people are
talking about because if clubs--

00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:14.560
it's a weird--

00:41:14.560 --> 00:41:16.240
it's weird to be
in a public-facing industry.

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:18.015
But if clubs leak it to

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:21.720
some media organization,
it's a non-zero chance.

00:41:21.720 --> 00:41:23.895
It makes it way
back to our group.

00:41:25.515 --> 00:41:26.850
If you're familiar
with football,

00:41:26.850 --> 00:41:30.330
one of the things that
last year started early in the

00:41:30.330 --> 00:41:33.120
season is teams would do what's
called a push quarterback

00:41:33.120 --> 00:41:35.670
sneak, where you would
have quarterback line up.

00:41:35.670 --> 00:41:37.790
And traditionally, they
would sneak forward. Now,

00:41:37.790 --> 00:41:40.710
you put somebody behind him and
two or three people are pushing.

00:41:40.710 --> 00:41:43.790
So similarly, quarterback
slide process, we were like,

00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:46.570
let's collect this data because
we saw it on ProFootballTalk in

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:49.630
week 2 or 3. And it's like,
if we don't collect it,

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:51.640
they're going to get to the
end of the year and complain about

00:41:51.640 --> 00:41:53.170
it. And it's going
to be like, oh, crap,

00:41:53.170 --> 00:41:55.130
we should have measured
this. So it's like, OK,

00:41:55.130 --> 00:41:56.450
now we got to measure it.

00:41:56.450 --> 00:41:58.945
So it's just being in tune
with what's out there,

00:41:59.630 --> 00:42:00.820
what we're seeing on the field.

00:42:00.820 --> 00:42:01.600
I mentioned--

00:42:01.600 --> 00:42:02.605
I know, Rachael, you asked--

00:42:03.130 --> 00:42:04.480
what I like to do for fun.

00:42:05.230 --> 00:42:06.640
I watch football for fun.

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:09.550
But I also watch it knowing
that whenever something

00:42:09.550 --> 00:42:11.500
happens, like sometimes,

00:42:11.500 --> 00:42:13.240
there's going to be a question
we need answer on Monday

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:15.460
morning. How often does this
happen? Is this getting worse?

00:42:16.390 --> 00:42:17.980
And addressing that.

00:42:17.980 --> 00:42:21.205
We deal with a good number of
questions from the clubs and from teams.

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:23.930
Sometimes from an
education perspective,

00:42:23.930 --> 00:42:25.534
but then also sometimes from a

00:42:26.956 --> 00:42:29.515
complaint perspective where
they think they're being--

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:33.580
there's a perceived
slide against them. So

00:42:34.570 --> 00:42:37.910
just trying to be a little
bit aware of what's going on.

00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:39.310
And for us, honestly,

00:42:39.310 --> 00:42:41.775
that's just paying
attention and watching. RACHAEL

00:42:41.775 --> 00:42:44.110
DEMPSEY: Thank you. Kayla,

00:42:44.110 --> 00:42:47.890
I see you had a question a
bit earlier as well about the types

00:42:47.890 --> 00:42:50.275
of tools they use. Do
you want to jump in here?

00:42:51.250 --> 00:42:54.040
- Sure. Yeah, I think I
asked a question about,

00:42:54.040 --> 00:42:56.490
if most of this work is
done in Python and R,

00:42:56.490 --> 00:42:58.300
are there any particular
packages if so,

00:42:58.300 --> 00:42:59.980
or if that goes
mostly internal?

00:42:59.980 --> 00:43:03.520
Or if there's any other
software typically used in NFL

00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:05.320
sports analytics?

00:43:05.320 --> 00:43:06.100
- A great--

00:43:06.100 --> 00:43:06.730
good question.

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:11.500
I would say, we're more on the
R than the Python side, but it's

00:43:12.340 --> 00:43:13.435
a decent mix.

00:43:14.590 --> 00:43:18.160
I've asked this to NFL
team staffers. And one other, I

00:43:18.160 --> 00:43:19.810
think, good point that just

00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:23.500
exists is that teams are
hiring more and more,

00:43:23.500 --> 00:43:26.830
so there's more and more folks
with our Python backgrounds going to teams.

00:43:27.850 --> 00:43:29.710
When I started, I want to say,

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:33.160
that there were maybe 70
or so data scientists.

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:35.790
Data scientists being a

00:43:36.470 --> 00:43:37.460
catch-all term here.

00:43:38.400 --> 00:43:42.020
Folks that were using football data to
try and help the team on the field.

00:43:43.940 --> 00:43:44.930
Maybe 70 or so.

00:43:44.930 --> 00:43:47.210
And I think the most
recent list is we're somewhere around

00:43:47.210 --> 00:43:50.720
130 or 140. So within
the last five years,

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:53.780
teams have basically doubled
their infrastructure on the

00:43:53.780 --> 00:43:54.530
football data side.

00:43:55.670 --> 00:43:59.640
And of that group, being familiar
with a lot of them, our

00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:01.220
team probably knows

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:03.860
the majority of those folks,

00:44:03.860 --> 00:44:06.455
I'd probably put it about
70-30 R versus Python.

00:44:07.220 --> 00:44:10.234
And that's probably where
our team sits internally.

00:44:13.070 --> 00:44:14.450
Certainly, there are--

00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:16.310
I think right now,

00:44:16.310 --> 00:44:18.973
most postings are like
have to know R or Python.

00:44:19.880 --> 00:44:24.050
A good number of managers are
probably more comfortable in R because of

00:44:24.770 --> 00:44:26.040
where football data has been,

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:29.330
but that doesn't necessarily
mean that it's any better.

00:44:30.210 --> 00:44:32.450
And then I also think there's
a growing number of roles for

00:44:32.450 --> 00:44:35.730
people that know neither, but
know what football data is.

00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:40.060
And this would be
your communicator type role.

00:44:40.060 --> 00:44:43.590
So folks that maybe are
really up-to-date with current

00:44:43.590 --> 00:44:47.100
football metrics and maybe
have coaching experience or playing

00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:50.160
experience, and they want
to be the translator.

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:53.610
They want to be the person that
is going and saying, hey, I

00:44:54.330 --> 00:44:56.400
can't fit that
neural net model,

00:44:56.400 --> 00:44:59.300
but I know you can and I know
what it's going to give me as a

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:00.480
result. And this is, I think,

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:03.675
what's going to help this coach
or this assistant GM or whatever.

00:45:04.510 --> 00:45:05.910
So I would even say,

00:45:05.910 --> 00:45:09.300
there's even a good amount
of room in football analytics for

00:45:09.300 --> 00:45:11.220
non-coders. People who

00:45:11.850 --> 00:45:15.870
have some ability to know
what's going on in the

00:45:15.870 --> 00:45:18.750
background, know a little
bit about modeling,

00:45:18.750 --> 00:45:21.330
and know what the strengths
and weaknesses are,

00:45:21.330 --> 00:45:24.750
while maybe not being able to
do it on player tracking data,

00:45:25.380 --> 00:45:28.590
but also having that ability.

00:45:28.590 --> 00:45:31.710
You really have to know
football to get into that role,

00:45:31.710 --> 00:45:33.066
and not every team has them.

00:45:34.770 --> 00:45:36.990
But if you stay up-to-date on
the Big Data Bowl and on Next

00:45:36.990 --> 00:45:39.900
Gen Stats and on
public-facing research,

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:42.810
you can provide a lot of
value to your team in terms of

00:45:42.810 --> 00:45:45.150
identifying trends that are
meaningful and maybe trends

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:46.230
that you would want to ignore. RACHAEL

00:45:46.230 --> 00:45:48.990
DEMPSEY: That's really cool. What
would that role be,

00:45:48.990 --> 00:45:50.130
the translator?

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:52.065
- I mean, if--

00:45:52.065 --> 00:45:53.320
so for example,

00:45:53.320 --> 00:45:55.260
you're an offensive coordinator
for a team and you want to

00:45:55.260 --> 00:45:56.250
build a scouting report,

00:45:56.250 --> 00:45:58.180
you want to build a
dashboard that says,

00:45:58.180 --> 00:46:01.170
here's what the opposing defense
is going to do each week,

00:46:02.250 --> 00:46:05.265
there are lots of data
scientists that could do that.

00:46:05.970 --> 00:46:07.060
A good number of them,

00:46:07.060 --> 00:46:09.450
especially the ones that have
spent the last four or five

00:46:09.450 --> 00:46:13.590
years in football tracking data
might not know the names that

00:46:13.590 --> 00:46:16.320
your team is using for what
that opposing defense is

00:46:16.320 --> 00:46:19.440
running. They might be able
to separate man from zone coverage,

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:23.520
but they might not be able to separate
cover 2 from cover 4 from cover 6.

00:46:23.520 --> 00:46:26.250
Or they might not be
able to understand the strong side

00:46:26.250 --> 00:46:28.240
linebacker for the
weak side linebacker.

00:46:28.240 --> 00:46:30.060
And so now, you need
somebody to say,

00:46:30.910 --> 00:46:32.970
OK, this is what the
coach is looking for,

00:46:32.970 --> 00:46:35.460
this is what this term is,
this is what this blitz is.

00:46:35.460 --> 00:46:39.180
And then your job is to code
it all and to work with that

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:41.430
communicator to help that.

00:46:42.570 --> 00:46:45.750
- I guess I was just curious,
like what that title is?

00:46:45.750 --> 00:46:48.090
How do people find those roles?

00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:49.500
-

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:53.250
A lot of them would just
be analysts or even quality control coach.

00:46:54.930 --> 00:46:58.140
Seth Walder from ESPN on
Twitter has a really good link.

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:00.210
I'm sure somebody can find
it and put it in the chat.

00:47:01.710 --> 00:47:03.600
All sports analytics
careers are like this.

00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:06.450
There's a bunch of different names for
people that are doing the same stuff.

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:09.150
And it's weird.

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:14.940
My title is director or senior
director of football data and analytics.

00:47:15.930 --> 00:47:19.630
I could be senior director of
football analytics research or a senior data--

00:47:19.630 --> 00:47:21.300
probably not a senior
data scientist anymore because I

00:47:21.300 --> 00:47:22.320
don't code as much. But

00:47:23.055 --> 00:47:26.730
some are analysts, some are
data scientists, some are whatever.

00:47:26.730 --> 00:47:27.230
And

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:30.520
there's not exactly one perfect

00:47:31.260 --> 00:47:34.725
name for it, but it's probably
an analyst type of role.

00:47:35.650 --> 00:47:37.605
But it is, a lot
of times, like--

00:47:39.010 --> 00:47:40.270
football coaches know a lot,

00:47:40.270 --> 00:47:42.190
and they're using data
even if they're not

00:47:42.910 --> 00:47:44.470
telling you they're using data.

00:47:45.100 --> 00:47:45.600
They're

00:47:46.420 --> 00:47:48.040
going to tell you that they're
going to target the left

00:47:48.040 --> 00:47:50.140
cornerback on the other team
because they turn their hips

00:47:50.140 --> 00:47:51.190
too early. Well,

00:47:51.190 --> 00:47:53.740
you could probably use an
analyst to go figure out when

00:47:53.740 --> 00:47:55.810
they turn their hips too
early, what does that mean?

00:47:55.810 --> 00:47:59.447
And so a lot of times,
you're just trying to communicate to be

00:48:00.730 --> 00:48:04.480
that line. Because
unfortunately or fortunately,

00:48:04.480 --> 00:48:07.150
that offensive coordinator
might not have all the time in

00:48:07.150 --> 00:48:08.240
the world for this

00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:10.940
person to advise them on it.

00:48:10.940 --> 00:48:13.210
So that's where you need
some of the translators. RACHAEL

00:48:13.210 --> 00:48:14.410
DEMPSEY: Well,
thank you.

00:48:15.220 --> 00:48:17.320
George, I see you had a
question in the chat a few

00:48:17.320 --> 00:48:18.910
minutes ago. Do you
want to jump in?

00:48:18.910 --> 00:48:19.770
- Yeah, thanks.

00:48:20.530 --> 00:48:22.270
So I was just wondering--

00:48:22.270 --> 00:48:24.400
this is something I
always think about whenever I'm

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:25.780
watching a sporting event--

00:48:25.780 --> 00:48:26.280
is

00:48:26.920 --> 00:48:27.985
how do I get

00:48:29.170 --> 00:48:32.760
my organization to
report at the level of

00:48:33.580 --> 00:48:38.590
what I see the NFL or
ESPN or any in-game commentator reporting on?

00:48:38.590 --> 00:48:41.590
The, kind of, analytics that
come out of their mouths,

00:48:41.590 --> 00:48:46.900
I'm just in awe of. And
it's historical. Sometimes, it's like, well,

00:48:46.900 --> 00:48:50.140
the last quarterback who did this
was in 1978 and he had

00:48:50.770 --> 00:48:53.530
blonde hair and went to
this college. And I'm like,

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:56.920
I mean, first of all,
I don't even think that's relevant.

00:48:56.920 --> 00:49:00.070
But the fact that they could
pull that out of nowhere in 10

00:49:00.070 --> 00:49:01.690
seconds is pretty impressive.

00:49:02.440 --> 00:49:05.785
I've tried doing that in
my field in HR in the past,

00:49:06.790 --> 00:49:11.050
and there's always the bar that
I have. But I'm just curious,

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:18.340
how does one go about even
trying to achieve that level of analytics?

00:49:19.300 --> 00:49:21.430
- It's almost like
a research team.

00:49:21.430 --> 00:49:23.470
And the NFL does have
a research team that

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:28.510
before every game, they provide
the media with an enormous--

00:49:30.010 --> 00:49:32.620
basically like a folder

00:49:33.400 --> 00:49:34.510
of all the things.

00:49:34.510 --> 00:49:38.680
And this is automatically
generated using various tools

00:49:39.340 --> 00:49:42.235
of all the things that
are applicable to these teams.

00:49:43.060 --> 00:49:46.840
And it's almost like scouting reports
that you would see of a

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:48.580
baseball player or
a football player.

00:49:48.580 --> 00:49:50.800
And so when a wide
receiver catches the ball,

00:49:50.800 --> 00:49:53.560
the commentator is able to go
and say, Oh, this is interesting. He

00:49:54.190 --> 00:49:55.180
caught the

00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:59.245
highest red zone target rate
of any tight end last year.

00:50:00.040 --> 00:50:02.270
And a lot of it's not
because they've done it.

00:50:02.270 --> 00:50:04.580
It's because somebody
has handed that to them.

00:50:04.580 --> 00:50:06.040
We don't do a lot with it.

00:50:06.880 --> 00:50:08.620
That's maybe certainly
more on the media side.

00:50:09.850 --> 00:50:12.190
But I do know that
that's the research.

00:50:12.190 --> 00:50:17.140
A media team's job is to prep
basically a portfolio for them to pull from.

00:50:18.310 --> 00:50:20.810
And I do think one of
the things that they're balancing is,

00:50:20.810 --> 00:50:24.070
how do I take a lot of the
new metrics that nobody's ever

00:50:24.070 --> 00:50:26.140
heard of? Versus some
of the old things,

00:50:26.140 --> 00:50:29.603
like everybody knows what a
blonde hair is. That they could--

00:50:29.603 --> 00:50:32.690
you could pull from that that
maybe wouldn't make as much sense.

00:50:32.690 --> 00:50:33.190
So

00:50:33.950 --> 00:50:35.150
that's where, I think,

00:50:35.150 --> 00:50:38.160
football data is now is how
much is too much and what

00:50:39.020 --> 00:50:41.120
is a reasonable thing
to give our audience?

00:50:41.120 --> 00:50:43.130
Maybe an area we don't
deal as much with,

00:50:43.130 --> 00:50:45.970
but it is relevant to some
of the research they get.

00:50:45.970 --> 00:50:48.320
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I realize
I missed a question over on

00:50:48.320 --> 00:50:50.555
Slido. So I want to make
sure I get to some of the--

00:50:50.555 --> 00:50:52.610
one of the anonymous
ones. But it was--

00:50:53.390 --> 00:50:57.150
you're going to have to help
me with this acronym. Does gait, G-A-I-T,

00:50:57.950 --> 00:51:00.710
analysis of the players
have any impact on their

00:51:00.710 --> 00:51:01.730
performance?

00:51:01.730 --> 00:51:04.970
And what are the KPIs that
you generally look for for a

00:51:04.970 --> 00:51:06.365
player's potential impact?

00:51:06.365 --> 00:51:07.235
- So

00:51:08.420 --> 00:51:10.970
we've tended to stay away
from some of the performance metrics.

00:51:11.600 --> 00:51:14.860
And I think that's what G-A-I-T would be.

00:51:15.830 --> 00:51:20.750
And so I wouldn't be able
to know as much there. I think

00:51:20.750 --> 00:51:23.810
the weird angle we would
come at performance would be like,

00:51:23.810 --> 00:51:27.740
how does the league set
policies or rules for a

00:51:27.740 --> 00:51:31.670
schedule or for practices that
allow players to reach some

00:51:31.670 --> 00:51:32.430
level of performance?

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:35.703
So we've done a lot
of research on, for example,

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:39.980
what is the impact of playing a
team that just had a bye week

00:51:39.980 --> 00:51:43.190
the previous week? Each team
gets one bye week a season.

00:51:43.190 --> 00:51:46.730
If you're the Jets and
you're playing the Giants,

00:51:46.730 --> 00:51:48.935
and the Giants have had
two weeks of practice,

00:51:51.470 --> 00:51:53.950
what type of advantage does
that provide you on the field?

00:51:54.710 --> 00:51:56.420
So it's a different
performance angle.

00:51:57.510 --> 00:52:00.920
And the other ones would
be like back-to-back-to-back road games or

00:52:02.330 --> 00:52:02.830
travel

00:52:03.620 --> 00:52:06.740
advantages. That's a different
type of performance thing,

00:52:06.740 --> 00:52:09.290
but I think that's probably the
closest to the thing that the

00:52:09.290 --> 00:52:13.400
league could control. Ultimately, for
a lot of legal reasons,

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:17.000
we're not as in tune with some
of the performance stuff on the team side

00:52:17.600 --> 00:52:18.440
at least the league office.

00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:21.680
But I do know the
teams are obviously interested in it.

00:52:21.680 --> 00:52:22.640
- Well, thank you.

00:52:24.050 --> 00:52:27.620
Brittany, I don't think we
got to your question yet.

00:52:27.620 --> 00:52:28.595
Do you want to jump in here?

00:52:30.050 --> 00:52:31.114
I think you had a--

00:52:31.114 --> 00:52:33.560
oh, you unmuted.
I'll have you ask it.

00:52:34.400 --> 00:52:35.510
- Yeah. So

00:52:36.500 --> 00:52:39.350
I know that with the
introduction of the RFID tags,

00:52:39.350 --> 00:52:40.290
I'm sure that there's

00:52:40.970 --> 00:52:44.660
a bunch of new data points
that you're exploring that

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:47.000
previously haven't been
explored. But also,

00:52:47.000 --> 00:52:50.780
I'm sure that there are maybe
changes to rules of thumb or

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:54.290
heuristics or around already
existing data points that maybe

00:52:54.290 --> 00:52:57.560
are coming into play now that you
have a little bit more specific data.

00:52:57.560 --> 00:53:02.000
Can you talk through how
you've been evangelizing those new and/or

00:53:02.720 --> 00:53:04.940
maybe different than expected
things and how you've been

00:53:04.940 --> 00:53:07.250
getting buy-in from your
business partners around that? MIKE LOPEZ

00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:08.180
: Good question.

00:53:11.750 --> 00:53:13.040
And I can share a
little bit of this

00:53:14.030 --> 00:53:17.855
because they shared it this
past offseason. But one area was--

00:53:18.410 --> 00:53:20.690
stuff I was just mentioning is
that we did a lot of research

00:53:20.690 --> 00:53:23.360
on what I would call
scheduling inequities and

00:53:24.140 --> 00:53:28.950
basically how things that the
league sets impact game outcomes.

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:31.200
And there were a good
number of surprising ones,

00:53:31.200 --> 00:53:33.510
where things that I think
most people would perceive as having

00:53:33.510 --> 00:53:37.110
a big impact on a team's chance
of winning the game maybe

00:53:37.110 --> 00:53:39.880
didn't matter as much. And so

00:53:40.560 --> 00:53:41.060
the

00:53:41.700 --> 00:53:44.130
scheduling team mentioned that
when they made their schedule

00:53:44.130 --> 00:53:46.875
this year, they took some
of our advice to heart.

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:50.640
And a lot of that,
honestly, was just trust.

00:53:51.660 --> 00:53:54.630
I think we had to do a
really thorough job of looking at all

00:53:54.630 --> 00:53:57.060
possible metrics that they
thought they might want.

00:53:57.060 --> 00:53:58.830
So they didn't say, go
look at x, y, and z.

00:53:59.730 --> 00:54:02.100
We thought from our
football background,

00:54:02.100 --> 00:54:06.120
what are the possible ways
that anybody out there could ever

00:54:06.120 --> 00:54:08.365
see an on-field impact
of these things?

00:54:09.270 --> 00:54:12.720
And when we presented,
it was thorough.

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:17.890
It had every possible angle. And
I think because of that approach

00:54:18.630 --> 00:54:20.620
where it's saying, like, hey,

00:54:20.620 --> 00:54:22.570
we're not going to give you
one thing and say, all right,

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:24.510
we're done. We solved
it. It's going to be,

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:26.970
we're going to give you a
bunch of different ways of looking at

00:54:26.970 --> 00:54:28.820
it, a couple of
different outcomes,

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:31.470
a couple different time points.

00:54:32.190 --> 00:54:33.210
We're also going to

00:54:33.960 --> 00:54:36.345
acknowledge our limitations
and things we don't know.

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:37.740
So for example,

00:54:38.640 --> 00:54:42.120
it might not be the case
that teams that are playing three

00:54:42.120 --> 00:54:44.475
road games in a row in
the NFL lose more often.

00:54:45.180 --> 00:54:48.300
But it is the case that if
you're on a team and you play

00:54:48.300 --> 00:54:50.880
three road games, you're away
from your family for three weeks.

00:54:50.880 --> 00:54:53.790
And that doesn't just mean that
we can go throw teams on the

00:54:53.790 --> 00:54:55.410
road for three, four, or five,

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:57.450
six weeks in a row
because that's going to have other

00:54:57.450 --> 00:54:59.610
impacts that maybe
we're not measuring.

00:54:59.610 --> 00:55:04.260
So it's not a great answer,
Britney. But honestly, it's just trust.

00:55:04.260 --> 00:55:08.100
It's building up a repertoire of
trying to look at a bunch of

00:55:08.100 --> 00:55:10.305
different things in a
bunch of different ways.

00:55:11.110 --> 00:55:12.390
There isn't--

00:55:12.390 --> 00:55:13.350
it's very rare.

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:15.240
I hope for my--

00:55:15.240 --> 00:55:16.980
like I said, I
have a great team.

00:55:17.680 --> 00:55:19.620
We're almost always
saying, yes. Like,

00:55:19.620 --> 00:55:21.570
can you also look at
this? Yep, we'll do it.

00:55:22.110 --> 00:55:23.110
Sometimes, maybe it's like,

00:55:23.110 --> 00:55:24.930
I don't really think that's
going to matter. But honestly,

00:55:24.930 --> 00:55:26.880
it doesn't matter. They think
it matters, so let's go find

00:55:26.880 --> 00:55:30.780
out. And so a lot of it is just
sometimes just saying, yep,

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:32.730
we're going to look at every
possible angle and try a bunch

00:55:32.730 --> 00:55:33.255
of different things.

00:55:34.245 --> 00:55:35.745
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I know that

00:55:36.510 --> 00:55:40.030
this hour is always the fastest
hour for me of the week.

00:55:40.030 --> 00:55:43.275
So let me rapid fire maybe two
more questions at you, if that's OK.

00:55:44.430 --> 00:55:47.340
Well, actually, this is a
quick one. Travis asked,

00:55:47.340 --> 00:55:49.260
have you ever lost
in fantasy football?

00:55:50.440 --> 00:55:52.290
- Fantasy football
is very noisy.

00:55:52.290 --> 00:55:54.400
You could be the best
fantasy football player in the world.

00:55:54.400 --> 00:55:58.330
And if you're in a team with
a league with 10 players,

00:55:58.330 --> 00:56:02.110
you probably didn't improve
your chances much more from 10%

00:56:02.110 --> 00:56:03.840
to 20%. I've lost plenty.

00:56:03.840 --> 00:56:06.760
We also have very strict restrictions
about what we can and can't play.

00:56:06.760 --> 00:56:09.420
So I'm going to just note that
I used to play it a lot more

00:56:09.420 --> 00:56:11.275
than I'm allowed to now.

00:56:11.275 --> 00:56:11.930
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I see.

00:56:13.370 --> 00:56:15.380
One other question
for you then.

00:56:16.250 --> 00:56:18.590
We'd love to hear
what's something that you're most

00:56:18.590 --> 00:56:21.830
excited about as you think
about the year ahead?

00:56:21.830 --> 00:56:22.790
MIKE LOPEZ : Oh gosh!

00:56:25.900 --> 00:56:26.400
Wow!

00:56:27.350 --> 00:56:28.270
I think-- RACHAEL

00:56:28.270 --> 00:56:30.070
DEMPSEY: It doesn't have to
be the biggest thing.

00:56:30.070 --> 00:56:32.095
Maybe it's the first thing that
comes to mind for your team.

00:56:33.110 --> 00:56:33.380
-

00:56:33.380 --> 00:56:35.770
I think the general state of
football is in a really good

00:56:35.770 --> 00:56:39.790
spot. Last year, we had the
lowest margin of victory since,

00:56:39.790 --> 00:56:40.930
I think, 1932.

00:56:42.340 --> 00:56:42.840
The

00:56:43.420 --> 00:56:46.570
game itself, a lot
of the analytic-driven trends--

00:56:46.570 --> 00:56:49.030
and I saw a couple of questions
in the chat about these.

00:56:49.030 --> 00:56:52.555
Teams are going for it on
fourth down more often. Teams are

00:56:53.470 --> 00:56:55.540
coming up with more
creative offenses.

00:56:55.540 --> 00:56:57.805
All of that has led to a
more entertaining football.

00:56:58.420 --> 00:56:58.690
I mean,

00:56:58.690 --> 00:57:02.020
I don't think anybody has ever
turned on a football game and

00:57:02.020 --> 00:57:03.410
been like, Oh, this is
going to be awesome.

00:57:03.410 --> 00:57:04.555
I can't wait to
see this punt play.

00:57:05.500 --> 00:57:06.775
So in general,

00:57:07.720 --> 00:57:10.480
the things that are happening
to football is making it more

00:57:10.480 --> 00:57:13.195
exciting to watch. And I
do think that that's created--

00:57:14.590 --> 00:57:15.820
it's always been
an entertaining product,

00:57:15.820 --> 00:57:18.280
but it's really at its
peak. So I think honestly,

00:57:18.980 --> 00:57:23.005
the trend for the game
itself is in good shape.

00:57:23.620 --> 00:57:24.813
And that,

00:57:25.700 --> 00:57:27.905
I think, continues to make
me excited about the season.

00:57:29.190 --> 00:57:31.530
It's always the case that
because you have a small sample

00:57:31.530 --> 00:57:33.450
size of games,
we have 17 games,

00:57:34.530 --> 00:57:35.650
surprises are going to happen.

00:57:35.650 --> 00:57:37.500
You're going to see new
teams that weren't in there.

00:57:37.500 --> 00:57:39.190
And that's always been
a part of football.

00:57:39.190 --> 00:57:42.990
But I just think it's a fun
time to be in a sport where a

00:57:42.990 --> 00:57:46.500
lot of the trends
are really improving the entertainment

00:57:46.500 --> 00:57:47.430
product of the league.

00:57:48.750 --> 00:57:51.590
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Absolutely. Well,
thank you so much, Mike,

00:57:51.590 --> 00:57:55.250
for joining us today and
sharing your experience with us

00:57:55.250 --> 00:57:57.510
all. And thank you all for
all the great questions.

00:57:57.510 --> 00:57:59.160
There were a ton
of questions here.

00:57:59.160 --> 00:58:01.250
I'm sorry if we
missed anybody's.

00:58:01.250 --> 00:58:03.950
I'll try and go through
and see what we missed.

00:58:03.950 --> 00:58:06.220
But have a great rest
of the day, everybody. MIKE LOPEZ

00:58:06.220 --> 00:58:09.020
: Thanks. And I left my
email in the chat,

00:58:09.020 --> 00:58:11.060
if anybody has any
follow-ups or concerns,

00:58:11.060 --> 00:58:13.820
or has a student or a friend
that wants to get into football data.

00:58:14.930 --> 00:58:16.940
Please don't hesitate
to reach out.

00:58:17.866 --> 00:58:19.670
- That's great.
Thanks so much, Mike.

00:58:20.610 --> 00:58:22.350
Have an awesome rest of
the day, everybody Bye.

